{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>Thin, flaky</b>. According to Onkelos (Onkelos on Exodus 16:14), it is from \"מַחְשֹׂף הַלָּבָן\" (Genesis 30:37), \"חָשַׂף YHWH\" (Isaiah 52:10), with the interchange of שי\"ן and סמ\"ך, and the ayin of the root is doubled:" "<b>Thin as frost</b> — this is the hoarfrost that falls in days of cold, as it says, \"כְּפוֹר כָּאֵפֶר יְפַזֵּר\" (Psalms 147:16). And Onkelos translated (Onkelos on Exodus 16:14): \"דַּעְדַק כְּגִיר כִּגְלִידָא עַל אַרְעָא\" (fine as gypsum, like ice, upon the earth). And Rashi wrote (Rashi on Exodus 16:14), of blessed memory: like gypsum stones, and it is a type of black dye, as we say in the chapter concerning covering the blood (Ḥullin 88b): \"the גִּיר and the זַּרְנִיךְ.\" דַּעְדַק כְּגִיר דִּגְלִידָא עַל אַרְעָא — it was fine as gypsum and lay congealed like ice upon the earth. And such is its meaning: <b>thin as frost</b>, spread out and liquid and congealed like a crust — thin, for it formed a thin crust above. And what Onkelos translates is an addition to the language of the Hebrew, which has no word for it in the verse. But none of this is correct, for גִּיר is the white dust that adheres to stones; they crush it and plaster walls with lime, and it is very white and better than lime for plastering on walls, as it is written (Daniel 5:5), \"עַל גִּירָא דִּי כְתַל הֵיכְלָא\" (on the plaster of the wall of the palace). Therefore the manna is compared to it, for it is white and scattered upon the earth like crushed gypsum.\nNow Onkelos translated the word כְּפוֹר in two ways: he first derived it from \"וְכָפַרְתָּ אֹתָהּ מִבַּיִת וּמִחוּץ בַּכֹּפֶר\" (Genesis 6:14), and therefore said כְּגִיר, for with it one plasters and covers; and he also derived it from \"כְּפוֹר כָּאֵפֶר יְפַזֵּר\" (Psalms 147:16), which is the thin ice that descends in the cold, as he translates \"וְקֶרַח בַּלָּיְלָה\" (Genesis 31:40) — \"וּגְלִידָא נָחִיתָ עָלַי בְּלֵילְיָא\" (and ice descended upon me at night); and from it he derived a kind of thing, and that one is called גְּלִיד, as we learned in the Mishnah of Mikva'ot (7:1): These raise [the water level] but do not render it invalid — snow, hail, frost (כְּפוֹר), and ice (גְּלִיד). And thus Onkelos translates in two ways in many verses. But in the corrected texts of the Targum it is written: \"דַּעְדַק דְּגִיר כִּגְלִידָא עַל אַרְעָא,\" and its meaning is: fine lumps of clay like ice upon the earth — and this is the truth, for if it were from the word גִּיר it would have been translated כְּגִירָא דִּגְלִידָא, for so is the way of that language."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {1 "The phrase חומרים חומרים (rendered here 'lumps of clay') is textually difficult; read hyper-literally as 'clods, clods.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:17:13.083977480Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}